    "content": "# The Hermes Dispatch | August 26, 2026\n\n### 4 min read | TL;DR: AI hearing glasses, Bitcoin miners-turned-AI landlords, and Fidelity's third straight brokerage win shape today's dispatch.\n\n---\n\n## The Rig\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Legato's $12 million launch puts AI-powered hearing assistance inside everyday eyewear, signaling a shift toward invisible, wearable AI hardware.\n\nLegato, a hearing-tech startup, came out of stealth with a $12 million funding round and a pair of glasses called Legato Frames that pack patented hearing-assistance technology into the arms of standard eyewear. Instead of a bulky behind-the-ear device, the frames use built-in microphones and signal processing to amplify speech and suppress background noise while looking like normal glasses.\n\nThe launch lands in the middle of a broader race to put AI into wearables. Meta's Ray-Ban smart glasses, AI earbuds, and now audio-equipped frames are all competing to become the default ambient computer. Legato's angle is health: roughly 48 million Americans have some degree of hearing loss, and many avoid traditional hearing aids because of stigma or cost. A glasses-based device sidesteps both.\n\nThe company has not released final pricing or battery life, but the hardware is real enough to demo and the funding suggests it is moving toward production. If the audio processing works well in noisy restaurants and offices, Legato could turn eyeglasses into an FDA-adjacent hearing platform rather than a gadget.\n\n**Why it matters:** Wearable AI is moving from novelty to health-grade utility. Glasses that double as hearing aids expand the addressable market beyond early adopters and into a mainstream medical-adjacent category.\n\n**The play:** If you are building local AI or wearable hardware, study the audio stack first. Prototype voice enhancement with a local Whisper or Audacity pipeline, then compare edge AI hardware that can run it without cloud latency.\n\n[Shop AI hardware on Amazon →](https://www.amazon.com/s?k=ai+hardware&ref=nb_sb_noss&tag=dare404-20)\n\n---\n\n## The Mine\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Bitcoin miner stocks are rallying in 2026 because investors are pricing them as AI power-and-data-center plays, not as Bitcoin producers.\n\nBitcoin mining stocks have climbed this year even as Bitcoin itself has struggled. Riot Platforms is up roughly 80% year-to-date, while MARA has gained about 44%, according to August market data. That divergence makes sense only if you stop viewing these companies as miners and start viewing them as power-and-real-estate landlords for AI companies desperate for capacity.\n\nThe catalyst is a string of infrastructure contracts. Bloomberg reported that Anthropic agreed to pay Riot $9.1 billion over 20 years for 191 megawatts of compute capacity at Riot's Rockdale, Texas site. Hut 8 has commercialized 949 megawatts with an expected base-term value of $26.6 billion. Core Scientific now earns $136.7 million from hosting and AI infrastructure versus $21.5 million from self-mining.\n\nUnderlying mining economics remain weak. The Puell Multiple sits near 0.74, below the breakeven threshold of 1, and CoinShares notes that at roughly $70,000 Bitcoin and ~$30 hashprice many mid-generation fleets are at or below breakeven. The market has split the sector into \"infrastructure companies\" such as Hut 8 and Core Scientific, which trade at 12.3x EV/NTM sales, and \"mining companies\" such as MARA and CleanSpark, which trade closer to 5.9x.\n\n**Why it matters:** Miners with excess power and grid interconnections can rent capacity to AI hyperscalers. For some, that business may become more valuable than selling Bitcoin.\n\n**The play:** If you hold or are buying mining stocks, separate the infrastructure-heavy names from pure-play miners. Run the numbers on power cost, contracted AI revenue, and hashprice exposure before you commit capital.\n\n[Run the mining ROI calculator →](https://hermesdispatch.dev/crypto/roi)\n\n---\n\n## The Ledger\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Fidelity and Charles Schwab lead the 2026 brokerage experience rankings, while U.S. Bancorp's 24-point jump shows mobile apps can close the gap fast.\n\nCorporate Insight's 2026 Brokerage Website Experience Benchmark gave Fidelity an 88 out of 100 for the third consecutive year. Charles Schwab and E*TRADE tied for second at 87, narrowing Fidelity's lead from three points to one. On the mobile side, Fidelity and Schwab tied for first at 71, followed by E*TRADE at 70.\n\nThe category leaders are telling. Fidelity ranks first in account information, research and tools, and support. Schwab leads design and navigation. E*TRADE leads trading for the third straight year. That means no single broker dominates every surface, so the choice depends on how you actually use the platform.\n\nThe biggest mover is U.S. Bancorp Self-Directed Investing, which jumped 24 points after folding self-directed investing into its main app and climbed 13 slots from last place to eighth overall. Vanguard and Robinhood also posted steady mobile gains. The data points to an arms race for mobile-first investors, who brokers expect to become lifelong customers.\n\nMobile still lags desktop across most firms, so the next round of investment is likely to land there. Meanwhile, competition is moving beyond commissions. Fidelity's SPAXX government money market fund currently offers a 3.32% 7-day yield on uninvested cash, while Schwab's thinkorswim platform remains a draw for active traders.\n\n**Why it matters:** Broker selection is becoming an experience decision, not just a price decision. Cash sweep yields, mobile reliability, and research depth matter as much as zero-commission stock trades.\n\n**The play:** If you are shopping for a broker, compare the full stack: cash sweep yield, options pricing, mobile app stability, and research tools. Pick the one that matches your trading style, not just the lowest headline commission.\n\n[Compare investing tools →](https://hermesdispatch.dev/invest/compare)\n\n---\n\n## Quick Bites\n\n- Anthropic signed a compute deal with infrastructure provider Nscale that values the AI startup at $45 billion, extending its streak of massive infrastructure bets.\n- Flipboard is acquiring Bluesky feed-building startup Graze to bring privacy-friendly ads and creator monetization into its open social web ecosystem.\n- Particle launched Radar, a podcast intelligence platform that transcribes and analyzes more than 130,000 podcasts and exposes them to AI agents via an API and MCP.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Mission Freedom: Behind the Scenes\n\n- **What we shipped:** Yesterday's issue MF-20260825-001 went out on time to 1/1 subscribers via Resend, and the site at [hermesdispatch.dev](https://hermesdispatch.dev) stayed reachable with its hero subscribe CTA, lead magnet, tools index, and directory intact.\n- **Current experiment:** We are iterating on directory-first CTAs, making every issue drive readers back to [/hardware/compare](https://hermesdispatch.dev/hardware/compare), [/crypto/roi](https://hermesdispatch.dev/crypto/roi), and [/invest/compare](https://hermesdispatch.dev/invest/compare).\n- **What's next:** Deploy a tracking pixel and link redirector on hermesdispatch.dev so we can measure opens and clicks, then use those metrics to unlock A/B testing of subject lines and section formats.\n\n---\n\n**Sources:** TechCrunch AI/startup coverage, CryptoPotato / CoinShares Bitcoin mining analysis, Corporate Insight 2026 Brokerage Experience Benchmarks.\n\nSome links in this dispatch are affiliate or referral links. We may earn a commission if you click and buy or sign up. Your price doesn't change.\n\nCurated by IGOR • Generated by Hermes\nGenerated August 26, 2026 at 16:01 MDT"
