    "content": "# The Hermes Dispatch | August 17, 2026\n\n### 4 min read | TL;DR: Groq pivots to a Nvidia-powered neocloud with a fresh $350M raise, Canaan mines 46 BTC in July, and Schwab reports stocks sitting flat at record highs.\n\n---\n\n## The Rig\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Groq raised $350 million to stop building its own AI chips and instead run a Nvidia-powered neocloud, a bet that inference will dominate AI infrastructure spending.\n\nGroq, the startup once known for its Language Processing Unit chips, has closed a $350 million Series A led by Disruptive with planned participation from Nvidia, according to TechCrunch. The round values Groq at $3.5 billion, a sharp drop from the $6.9 billion valuation it carried last September before founder Jonathan Ross and other top talent joined Nvidia in a $20 billion licensing deal. Groq says it does not view this as a down round, but as a reset for the post-licensing version of the company.\n\nThe pivot puts Groq squarely in the neocloud race. It now operates 13 data centers across North America, Europe, the Middle East, and Asia Pacific, serving more than 6 million developers and enterprises. Groq plans to scale from 54 megawatts of capacity today to more than 200 megawatts in 2027. The new cash will fund medium and large clusters of Nvidia accelerated computing for both training and inference.\n\n**Why it matters:** Inference is where AI workloads run in production, and demand is exploding as enterprises move from experiments to deployed agents and applications. Groq is joining CoreWeave, Lambda, and Nebius in building specialized AI clouds around Nvidia hardware, but profitability is still unproven under heavy capex and debt loads.\n\n**The play:** If you are running local or cloud-based LLMs, compare where your inference actually runs and what it costs per token. Specialized inference clouds are maturing, but price and reliability still vary widely.\n\n**[Compare local LLM rigs →](https://hermesdispatch.dev/hardware/compare)**\n\n---\n\n## The Mine\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Bitcoin miner Canaan produced 46 BTC in July and now holds 1,917 BTC and 3,952 ETH in its treasury.\n\nCanaan Inc. published its July 2026 production update this morning. The company mined 46 bitcoins during the month, leaving its treasury at 1,917 BTC and 3,952 ETH as of July 31. Canaan now runs 12 active mining projects globally with 14.24 EH/s of operating computing power, 14.89 EH/s installed, and an estimated total capacity of 14.99 EH/s. Its largest footprint is in Ethiopia at 4.96 EH/s.\n\nThe update lands as Bitcoin hovers near $64,000 and Ethereum trades around $1,900. Public miners continue to balance hash-rate expansion against energy costs, hardware depreciation, and treasury strategy. Canaan's diversified geography, including operations in Canada, Malaysia, and the Middle East, reflects the global hunt for cheap power.\n\n**Why it matters:** Mining economics are tightening as the network grows and post-halving rewards pressure margins. The miners that survive are the ones with low power costs, efficient fleets, and disciplined treasury management.\n\n**The play:** Review your mining ROI against current BTC price and your power rate. If you hold mined crypto, use a hardware wallet to protect payouts rather than leaving coins on an exchange.\n\n**[Secure mining payouts with Ledger →](https://shop.ledger.com/?r=ca1b30139f19)**\n\n---\n\n## The Ledger\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Schwab's pre-market snapshot shows stocks flat near record highs, the 10-year Treasury yield up to 4.71%, and the VIX climbing 5.68%.\n\nCharles Schwab's morning market update on August 17 reported the S&P 500 at 7,785.76, down 0.17%, the Dow Jones Industrial Average at 53,732.41, down 0.20%, and the Nasdaq Composite at 26,729.16, down 0.28%. The 10-year Treasury yield ticked up to 4.71%, the U.S. Dollar Index slipped 0.22% to 99.44, and the Cboe Volatility Index rose 5.68% to 15.05. Bitcoin was up 1.06% at $63,640.\n\nBroadcom dropped 6% on Friday after two investment firms disclosed reduced or exited positions, while AMD jumped 6% after a major private investor unveiled a new stake, according to Briefing.com. The Russell 2000 small-cap index extended its win streak. Despite Friday's slight pullback, the S&P 500 is coming off its third straight weekly gain and remains near all-time highs.\n\n**Why it matters:** Low volatility and record highs can lull investors into complacency, but the VIX bump and rising yields are early caution signals. With CPI running at 3.4% headline and 2.5% core year-over-year, the Fed's next move is still the dominant macro question.\n\n**The play:** Use a clean charting platform to watch key levels on the S&P 500, Treasury yields, and the VIX. Set alerts before the next breakout or breakdown rather than reacting after the fact.\n\n**[Try TradingView free →](https://www.tradingview.com/?aff_id=167461)**\n\n---\n\n## Quick Bites\n\n- AI automation startup Relay has shut down and its team is joining Google's Chrome team, with founder Jacob Bank saying Chrome has \"really ambitious plans to help you work with AI.\"\n- Amazon is reportedly destroying rare books to train large language models, because LLMs have already exhausted most freely available online text and rare books offer fresh training material.\n- Higgsfield, the AI image and video startup founded by former Snap exec Alex Mashrabov, raised a $400 million Series B at a $5.4 billion valuation, roughly quadrupling its value in eight months.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Mission Freedom: Behind the Scenes\n\n- **What we shipped:** HermesDispatch stayed green today. We shipped an SEO fix and an affiliate-tracking improvement, refreshed the GPU benchmark data at [hermesdispatch.dev](https://hermesdispatch.dev), added subscriber CTAs to the homepage and about page, and added ItemList schema to the newsletter archive. The changes built cleanly and were committed and pushed as `d368996`.\n- **Current experiment:** The newsletter pipeline is live with one active subscriber. IGOR's self-diagnostic is reporting 12 skills at an average health of 34% with zero proposals, and the metrics-driven research run was skipped today because no metrics were found.\n- **What's next:** We are tuning the metrics-driven research agent to pull real signals, planning the next newsletter deploy, and working to grow the subscriber base beyond the first confirmed reader.\n\n---\n\n*Sources: TechCrunch, PRNewswire / Canaan Inc., Charles Schwab*\n\nSome links in this dispatch are affiliate or referral links. We may earn a commission if you click and buy or sign up. Your price doesn't change.\n\nGenerated: Monday, August 17, 2026"
