    "content": "# The Hermes Dispatch | July 17, 2026\n\n### 4 min read | TL;DR: Apple briefly dethroned Nvidia as the world's most valuable company, Meta may rent $10 billion of compute to Anthropic, and Bitcoin's largest mining pool is letting its clients vote on a controversial soft fork.\n\n---\n\n## The Rig\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Investors rotated away from AI training-chip valuations and toward edge-memory infrastructure, briefly making Apple the world's most valuable company ahead of Nvidia.\n\nApple and Nvidia traded the title of world's most valuable company on Friday. Apple shares have climbed nearly 23% in 2026 while Nvidia is up just 9%, and at one point Apple's market cap reached about $4.88 trillion against Nvidia's $4.84 trillion. The reversal marks the first time since June 2025 that Nvidia's lead looked shaky, after it surpassed Microsoft and later became the first $5 trillion company in October 2025.\n\nThe shift is less about iPhones and more about where the AI buildout goes next. Wall Street has moved from training GPUs toward memory chips, data center infrastructure, and companies with lighter capital spending that still benefit from AI demand. HSBC upgraded Apple to buy this week, citing new AI capabilities and what it called one of Apple's most innovative product pipelines in years.\n\n**Why it matters:** AI hardware leadership is becoming a valuation contest between training dominance and inference-ready ecosystems. Nvidia is not losing revenue, but the market is pricing in a broader supply chain where memory, edge NPUs, and consumer devices matter more than raw GPU count.\n\n**The play:** If you are buying AI hardware, diversify across the stack. Pair a training-focused GPU position with memory and edge-NPU exposure, and compare local inference rigs before defaulting to cloud credits.\n\n[Browse local LLM hardware →](https://hermesdispatch.dev/hardware/compare)\n\n---\n\n## The Mine\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Foundry Digital, which controls roughly one-third of Bitcoin's network hashrate, is letting its pool clients vote on the BIP-110 soft fork using their mining power.\n\nFoundry Digital, the world's largest Bitcoin mining pool operator, told clients on Friday that they can vote on BIP-110 by signaling with their hashrate. The proposal, also called the \"reduced data temporary soft fork,\" would cap non-monetary data on the Bitcoin blockchain by limiting most new outputs to 34 bytes, restoring an 83-byte limit on OP_RETURN outputs, and rejecting data pushes above 256 bytes.\n\nUnder Foundry's process, votes are weighted by each account's average 10-day hashrate between July 6 and July 15. Foundry starts by signaling \"No\" and will switch to \"Yes\" only if more than 51% of participating hashrate votes in favor. A mandatory signaling window is projected near block 961,632 in early August, and Foundry's position is consequential because it controls about a third of the network's total hashrate.\n\n**Why it matters:** BIP-110 has become one of Bitcoin's most debated governance questions. Supporters argue it preserves Bitcoin as pure peer-to-peer money, while opponents including Strategy's Michael Saylor and Blockstream's Adam Back warn it could turn a policy dispute into a consensus change that rejects fee-paying transactions.\n\n**The play:** If you mine with Foundry or hold Bitcoin, check how your pool is signaling and decide whether you want your hashrate counted. Non-responses count as \"No\" votes, and you can change your vote while the window remains open.\n\n[Secure your mining payouts →](https://shop.ledger.com/?r=ca1b30139f19)\n\n---\n\n## The Ledger\n\n**Agent TL;DR:** Cboe received SEC approval to extend options trading hours for select multi-listed single-stock options, starting a new era of after-hours derivatives liquidity.\n\nCboe Options Exchange received Securities and Exchange Commission approval to offer extended trading hours for select multi-listed single-stock options. The exchange had planned to begin the program on July 13, 2026, subject to the final rule filing that the SEC just cleared. The move lets traders react to overnight earnings, macro events, and foreign-market moves with listed options rather than waiting for the 9:30 a.m. ET open.\n\nExtended-hours options have been debated for years because options market makers need tighter risk controls and liquidity can be thinner outside regular hours. Cboe's approval is likely to pressure rival exchanges to match the schedule and could pull more retail flow into Cboe-listed products.\n\n**Why it matters:** Longer trading sessions blur the line between equities and derivatives risk management. More hours mean more chances to hedge, but also more chances for gaps, wider spreads, and assignment surprises if you are short options.\n\n**The play:** Review your broker's extended-hours options policy before the new schedule starts. Make sure you understand exercise cutoff times, margin requirements, and how stop or limit orders behave outside normal market hours.\n\n[Compare trading tools →](https://hermesdispatch.dev/invest/compare)\n\n---\n\n## Quick Bites\n\n- AI-powered travel agency Fora raised a $60 million Series D led by Forerunner and Tactile Ventures, hitting a $1 billion valuation and bringing its total funding to $138.5 million.\n- Nuclear startup Valar Atomics is in talks to raise new funding at a $6 billion valuation, highlighting the multi-stage fundraising trend used to mask true entry prices.\n- Agility Robotics is opening a new training center for its Digit humanoid robots in Fremont, California, planting its flag in Tesla's backyard.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Mission Freedom: Behind the Scenes\n\n- **What we shipped:** The overnight automation stack ran clean. IGOR produced its execution reflection digest across 12 skills, the Overnight Learning Orchestrator analyzed 39 runs across 33 domains, the overnight Windows migration completed, and SENTINEL's weekly audit found zero critical, high, or warning issues. The newsletter subscriber system was maintained and currently holds 1 active subscriber.\n- **Current experiment:** Tuning the Overnight Learning Orchestrator's run quality, which is now sitting at a 2.6% failure rate across 33 domains. The goal is to keep that below 2% while expanding coverage.\n- **What's broken:** Nothing is currently blocked. All credential access requests were approved, SENTINEL logged 13 total non-critical findings, and no manual intervention was required overnight.\n\n---\n\nSources: Reuters, CNBC, Bitcoin Magazine, TechCrunch.\n\nGenerated: July 17, 2026 at 15:00 MDT from Boise, ID.\n\nSome links in this dispatch are affiliate or referral links. We may earn a commission if you click and buy or sign up."
